<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="ai-policy">
  <title>AI Policy</title>

  <p>
    You may <alert>not</alert> use artificial intelligence (AI) tools to
    complete your assignments and assessments in this course.
  </p>

  <subsection xml:id="ai-tutor">
    <title>The Course AI Tutor</title>

    <p>
      The one exception is the tutor built into the course materials. Each of
      the ten assignment pages in the online skeletal notes carries a Socratic
      AI tutor <mdash/> see, for example, the
      <url href="https://mahmadi-ops.github.io/M13-Skeletal-Notes/worksheet-assignment-10.html">Assignment 10 page</url>. It is reachable from the
      <q>Tutor</q> button in the lower-right corner of the page, and it knows
      the problems on that page. It coaches you toward your own solution: it
      asks you questions, points you to the worked examples in the notes, and,
      when you show work that is wrong, tells you which step breaks and why.
    </p>

    <p>
      <alert>The tutor will not give you answers.</alert> It will not complete
      a solution or correct a wrong step for you. The one way to have an answer
      confirmed is to state your conclusion together with your own
      justification; a bare answer, right or wrong, earns nothing.
    </p>

    <p>
      You get <alert>three hints per problem, permanently</alert>. The budget
      never resets, and every reply from the tutor spends one. When your hints
      for a problem run out, bring your work to office hours.
    </p>

    <p>
      The review problem sets deliberately have no tutor. Those are practice
      for the exams, where you are on your own.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ai-tutor-key">
    <title>Setting Up the Tutor</title>

    <p>
      Using the tutor requires a free Google AI Studio key, which takes about a
      minute to create at
      <url href="https://aistudio.google.com/apikey">Google AI Studio</url>.
      Use a <alert>personal Google account, not your @scu.edu account</alert>
      <mdash/> the University has this service disabled. Your key is stored only
      in your own browser; the course site never sees it.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="ai-tutor-privacy">
    <title>Privacy</title>

    <p>
      Everything you type into the tutor goes to Google under Google's terms,
      not the University's, and on the free tier your conversations may be read
      by people at Google. Keep the chat to mathematics:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            No personal information <mdash/> no name, student ID, email, or
            phone number.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Nothing about a classmate.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>Nothing sensitive.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      The tutor is a study aid. It is not a counselor, and it is not the
      instructor.
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>
